\import{fnip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fnip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Classification by trace and determinant}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{In the plane the characteristic polynomial of a
[Jacobian](fnip-0034) is #{\lambda^{2}-\tau\lambda+\Delta}, where
#{\tau} is the trace and #{\Delta} is the determinant. The
discriminant is #{\tau^{2}-4\Delta}.}

\p{The signs of #{\tau}, #{\Delta}, and the discriminant name the
equilibrium. When #{\Delta<0} the eigenvalues are real and of
opposite sign, and the equilibrium is a [saddle](fnip-0035). When
#{0<\Delta<\tau^{2}/4} the eigenvalues are real and of the same
sign, and the equilibrium is a [node](fnip-0035), stable when
#{\tau<0} and unstable when #{\tau>0}. When #{\Delta>\tau^{2}/4}
the eigenvalues are a complex conjugate pair, and the equilibrium
is a [focus](fnip-0035), stable when #{\tau<0} and unstable when
#{\tau>0}.}

\p{Along #{\Delta=0} one eigenvalue is #{0}. Along #{\tau=0} with
#{\Delta>0} the eigenvalues are purely imaginary. In each of those
cases an eigenvalue has real part #{0}. [Jacobian](fnip-0034)
calls the equilibrium hyperbolic when every real part is nonzero,
so the names above apply off those two lines.}

\p{The upper drawing takes #{\Delta} horizontal and #{\tau}
vertical, as in
\citet{Figure 4.15, p. 104}{izhikevich2007dynamical}. The parabola
is #{\Delta=\tau^{2}/4}. The shaded region is #{\tau<0} and
#{\Delta>0}, the stable nodes and the stable foci. A filled circle
marks a stable equilibrium and an open circle an unstable
equilibrium.}

\p{The lower drawing enlarges a neighborhood of the origin and
marks the three foci of \ref{fnip-0037} and \ref{fnip-0038}.
\ref{fnip-0037} gives the Morris–Lecar equilibrium at
#{I_{\mathrm{app}}=60} and the equilibrium at
#{I_{\mathrm{app}}=100}. \ref{fnip-0038} gives the FitzHugh–Nagumo
origin. Each of these points lies to the right of the parabola, so
each equilibrium is a focus. The sign of the trace distinguishes the
two stable foci from the unstable one.}

